   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "a1e5c0d3",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Interpretation of the sign**\n",
    "\n",
    "| State | Z-basis parity | X-basis parity |\n",
    "|---|---|---|\n",
    "| Bell-plus $(\\lvert 00\\rangle+\\lvert 11\\rangle)/\\sqrt2$ | +1 | +1 |\n",
    "| Bell-minus $(\\lvert 00\\rangle-\\lvert 11\\rangle)/\\sqrt2$ | +1 | −1 |\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Positive sign (+1):** the two qubits always give the *same* result in that basis (perfect correlation).\n",
    "- **Negative sign (−1):** the two qubits always give *opposite* results in that basis (perfect anticorrelation).\n",
    "- **Zero:** the results are uncorrelated.\n",
    "\n",
    "In the Z basis, both Bell states have parity +1, because each state contains only `00` and `11`. The minus sign in Bell-minus is a phase, and Z-basis probabilities do not record phases. This is why Z measurements cannot distinguish the two states.\n",
    "\n",
    "In the X basis, the phase becomes visible. Applying H to both qubits maps Bell-plus to $(\\lvert 00\\rangle+\\lvert 11\\rangle)/\\sqrt2$, which still has parity +1. It maps Bell-minus to $(\\lvert 01\\rangle+\\lvert 10\\rangle)/\\sqrt2$, so its parity becomes −1. The sign of the X-basis parity therefore reads out the relative phase directly. In this ideal simulation they are exactly ±1.\n",
    "\n",
    "For comparison, the product state from the worked example has parity 0 in Z, because all four outcomes are equally likely. In X its parity is +1, because applying H to both qubits again returns it to $\\lvert 00\\rangle$. Being correlated in one basis is possible without entanglement. A Bell state, however, is perfectly correlated in both Z and X: parity ±1 in both bases. No product state can achieve this, which is the signature of entanglement."
